```

We have method `add(int index, E element)` that inserts element at index. The comment says: If index is between 0 and initial size inclusive, then for every valid index i < index, the element at position i after operation is either null if it was null before, or remains unchanged.

Essentially, for i from 0 to index-1, the element at i after operation equals the old element at i (maybe null case). Actually they say "either null if it was null before the operation, or remains unchanged." That's same as: the element after equals the old element (since if it was null before, it's null after). So we can assert that for all i with 0 <= i < index, this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) (or if null, use ==). In Java spec we can use .equals but need to handle null. Usually they use .equals for objects but need null safety. Could use \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). However many examples just use .equals and assume non-null. But here they explicitly mention null case. Might need to express with implication: (\old(this.get(i)) == null => this.get(i) == null) && (\old(this.get(i)) != null => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

Simplify: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) == null => this.get(i) == null) && (\old(this.get(i)) != null => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

Also need premise that index is between 0 and \old(this.size()) inclusive. So we need to assert: 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => (the forall...). Use =>.

Thus final assert:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i < index; (\old(this.get(i)) == null => this.get(i) == null) && (\old(this.get(i)) != null => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) );

Make sure syntax: \forall int i; cond; spec. The spec after ; is the condition. So we need parentheses.

Thus final answer in code block.

